Preparation

Modelling cognition: Select, implement, and evaluate a model for music cognition. The model must be evaluated against real human performance. Thus, data collection is necessary. 

Be prepared to discuss which theories of music perception and cognition the project relates to.
Research question
“What do you imagine when you listen to music?”

Prior motivation, which needs to be narrowed: I want people to tell me how they feel about the music they listen to. Not only feel but also what they imagine when listening to these tracks. Does it correlate with the speed of the music, the peaks and valleys, the genre, or anything else?

Tasks to help narrow this down:
I need to find out what I want to focus on. Is it the speed, the highs or lows, or something different.
What kinda tracks do I wanna play?

Suggestions:
I can use cards that people can pick from to describe their feeling and imaginations best. 
I can use PANAS-SF test to test mood after someone has listened to music.

Course description of question:
Systematic work on what kind of music structures and characteristics that evoke spontaneous visual imagery is still scarce but starting to gain attention (Taruffie & Küssner, 2019). So far, not much work has included both psychophysiological measures and qualitative reports. In this project, you can set up a listening test to investigate the kind of imagery (if any) and emotional and physiological response of listeners using psychophysiological measures as well as behavioral and self report. Alternatively, use the MIR toolbox to analyze music features of previous stimuli (e.g. Dahl et al. or Taruffie et al.), or own produced music and make predictions of reactions and self reports. 
